      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2011 The Authors. Colorectal Disease © 2011 The Association of Coloproctology of Great Britain and Ireland.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "AIM",
            "content": "This study was carried out to determine whether rectal bleeding is related to stage of bowel cancer and whether earlier diagnosis and treatment are associated with improved survival."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHOD",
            "content": "Eight hundred and forty-five patients were identified in the Wessex Bowel Cancer Audit (1991-1994). Presenting symptoms were identified from case notes. Outcome measures included 5-year survival, Dukes' stage, metastatic disease at surgery and time from onset of symptoms to treatment, in patients presenting with rectal bleeding or other symptoms and signs."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Six hundred and seventy-six (80%) of 845 patient case notes were reviewed. Of these, 408 (60.4%) patients had rectal or sigmoid cancer, and 255 (62.5%) of these 408 patients, who presented with rectal bleeding, had significantly earlier stage disease than those with a change in bowel habit and/or abdominal pain (Dukes' stage A: 23.1%vs 3.6%; Dukes' stage D: 14.5%vs 23.4%; P < 0.001), fewer metastases visible at surgery (14.9%vs 22.6%; P < 0.001) and significantly better 5-year survival (54.8%vs 40.9%; P < 0.001). There was no further significant improvement in 5-year survival in patients treated within 6 months of the onset of symptoms (55.1%vs 53.5%). Hazard ratios showed that 5-year survival was independently associated with age, Dukes' stage and emergency treatment, but not with rectal bleeding, change in bowel habit, abdominal pain or delay in treatment."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Bowel cancer patients presenting with rectal bleeding had earlier stage disease and significantly better survival than patients presenting with a change in bowel habit or abdominal pain. There was no reduction in 5-year survival in those patients who had a delay in treatment for > 6 months from the onset of symptoms."
          }
        ]
      },
